episode.caption:
Python threads share a single Global Interpreter Lock (GIL), meaning CPU-heavy tasks run strictly one at a time. To utilize multiple cores, swap ThreadPoolExecutor for ProcessPoolExecutor to run separate OS processes!

Have you been burned by Python thread limits in prod? Let us know below!

@prodmanor

In Node.js and browsers, setTimeout delays are stored as 32-bit signed integers. Any delay larger than 2147483647 ms (~24.8 days) overflows instantly to 1 ms, executing your callback right away!

Have you ever shipped a long setTimeout to prod? Tell us in the comments!

@prodmanor

Recursive functions rely on the process call stack, which in Python caps at 1000 calls. When processing arbitrary user-generated trees or graphs, swap recursion for an explicit heap-allocated list stack to handle infinite depth!

What is the deepest recursion bug you have seen in production?

@prodmanor

Calling f.readlines() or f.read() on large files forces Python to load the entire payload into RAM at once, triggering container OOM (Out Of Memory) kills. Iterate directly over the file generator `for line in f:` to stream chunks lazily!

